window.SEARCH=[{"t": "Linux", "u": "01-linux/index.html", "x": "DevOps ka 90% kaam Linux servers par hota hai. Yeh foundation strong karo, baaki sab tools iske upar chalte hain."}, {"t": "Linux: Deep Guide", "u": "01-linux/guide.html", "x": "DevOps ka 90% kaam Linux servers par hota hai. Yeh foundation strong karo, baaki sab tools iske upar chalte hain. **Problem it solves:** Almost every server, container, CI runner and cloud VM runs Linux. If you cannot operate Linux from the command line, you cannot deploy, debug or secure anything else in DevOps. Linux gives a stable, scriptable, free operating system where **everything is a file** and every task can be automated."}, {"t": "Filesystem & Basic Commands - Linux", "u": "01-linux/filesystem-commands.html", "x": "Linux mein sab kuch file hai. Root `/` se tree shuru hota hai. Pehle directory structure aur daily commands aani chahiye. pwd; ls -lah mkdir -p project/{src,logs,conf} cd project && touch src/app.py cp -r src src_backup mv src_backup /tmp/ rm -rf /tmp/src_backup # careful: no undo cat /etc/os-release head -n 5 /var/log/syslog; tail -f /var/log/syslog # live follow find /var/log -name \"*.log\" -mtime -1 -size +10M du -sh * | sort -h # what is eating space ln -s /opt/app/current /var/www/app # symlink (used in deployments) ls /etc | wc -l ps aux | grep nginx | grep -v grep echo \"hello\" > a.txt # "}, {"t": "Users, Groups & Permissions - Linux", "u": "01-linux/users-permissions.html", "x": "Linux multi-user OS hai. Har file ke owner, group aur others ke permissions hote hain: read(4), write(2), execute(1). Security ka base yahi hai. sudo adduser deploy sudo groupadd devs && sudo usermod -aG devs deploy sudo usermod -aG sudo deploy # sudo access id deploy; groups deploy chmod 750 deploy.sh # rwx r-x --- chmod +x script.sh; chmod -R g+w /var/www sudo chown -R www-data:www-data /var/www/app umask 022 # default: files 644, dirs 755 # special bits chmod u+s /usr/bin/x # setuid; chmod g+s dir (inherit group); chmod +t /tmp (sticky) setfacl -m u:anup:rwx /data/reports getfacl /data/repo"}, {"t": "Processes, Signals & systemd - Linux", "u": "01-linux/process-systemd.html", "x": "Running program = process. DevOps engineer ko services start/stop, hung process kill, aur logs (journalctl) dekhna aana chahiye. Modern Linux mein services systemd manage karta hai. ps aux --sort=-%mem | head top # or htop; press M sort by mem, P by cpu pgrep -a nginx kill -15 1234 # graceful; then kill -9 1234 if stuck nice -n 10 ./job.sh; renice 5 -p 1234 nohup ./long.sh > out.log 2>&1 & ; jobs ; fg # /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service [Unit] Description=My Node App After=network.target [Service] User=deploy WorkingDirectory=/opt/myapp ExecStart=/usr/bin/node server.js Restart=always RestartS"}, {"t": "Text Processing: grep, sed, awk - Linux", "u": "01-linux/text-processing.html", "x": "Logs aur config par kaam karne ke liye grep/sed/awk/cut/sort/uniq roz use hote hain. Incident ke time 5 minute mein answer nikalne ki power. awk '{print $1}' /var/log/nginx/access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head awk '$9 ~ /^5/ {print $7}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head grep -rn \"ERROR\" /var/log/app/ --include=\"*.log\" grep -E \"timeout|refused\" app.log grep -B2 -A5 \"Exception\" app.log # context lines zgrep \"ERROR\" app.log.*.gz sed -i 's/^port=.*/port=8080/' app.conf sed -n '10,20p' file; sed '/^#/d' file # print range; delete comments curl -s api/users | jq '.[] | select(.a"}, {"t": "Linux Networking & SSH - Linux", "u": "01-linux/networking-ssh.html", "x": "Server se connect karna, ports check karna, aur connectivity debug karna. SSH remote access ka standard hai, keys use karo, passwords nahi. ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C \"anup@laptop\" ssh-copy-id user@server # ~/.ssh/config Host prod HostName 10.0.1.5 User ubuntu IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 ProxyJump bastion # then just: ssh prod ssh -L 5432:db.internal:5432 bastion # local port forward to private DB scp file.tar.gz prod:/tmp/ ; rsync -avz --progress ./dist/ prod:/var/www/app/ ip a; ip route ss -tulpn # who listens on which port curl -v https://example.com/health dig +short example.com; dig @8.8"}, {"t": "Disk, Storage & Package Management - Linux", "u": "01-linux/disk-storage.html", "x": "Disk full ho jana production ka classic issue hai. Disks mount karna, LVM se resize, aur apt/yum se software manage karna aana chahiye. lsblk sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdb sudo mkdir /data && sudo mount /dev/sdb /data blkid /dev/sdb # copy UUID echo 'UUID=xxxx /data ext4 defaults,nofail 0 2' | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab sudo mount -a # test fstab BEFORE reboot sudo lvextend -L +10G /dev/vg0/data sudo resize2fs /dev/vg0/data # xfs_growfs /data for xfs df -hT; df -i sudo du -xh / --max-depth=2 | sort -h | tail sudo lsof +L1 # deleted files still held open sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y nginx apt l"}, {"t": "Performance & Troubleshooting - Linux", "u": "01-linux/performance-troubleshooting.html", "x": "Production issue mein systematic approach chahiye: CPU, memory, disk IO, network. USE method (Utilization, Saturation, Errors) follow karo. uptime; dmesg -T | tail vmstat 1 5; mpstat -P ALL 1 3 free -m; iostat -xz 1 3 sar -n DEV 1 3; ss -s; top -bn1 | head -15 strace -f -p <pid> -e trace=network,file lsof -p <pid> | head cat /proc/<pid>/status | grep -E 'VmRSS|Threads' sysctl net.core.somaxconn echo 'net.core.somaxconn=1024' | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/99-app.conf; sudo sysctl --system ulimit -n; # 'too many open files' -> raise LimitNOFILE in systemd unit"}, {"t": "Bash Scripting for DevOps - Linux", "u": "01-linux/bash-scripting.html", "x": "Repetitive kaam automate karne ke liye Bash script. Safe scripts likho: `set -euo pipefail`, quotes, functions, logging. #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail trap 'echo \"failed at line $LINENO\" >&2' ERR log() { echo \"[$(date +%F' '%T)] $*\"; } usage() { echo \"Usage: $0 <env>\"; exit 1; } [[ $# -eq 1 ]] || usage ENV=\"$1\" log \"Deploying to $ENV\" for host in $(cat hosts_$ENV.txt); do ssh \"$host\" 'sudo systemctl restart myapp' && log \"$host OK\" || log \"$host FAILED\" done #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail SRC=/var/www; DEST=/backup; KEEP=7 tar czf \"$DEST/site-$(date +%F).tar.gz\" \"$SRC\" find \"$DE"}, {"t": "Networking", "u": "02-networking/index.html", "x": "Deployment, load balancer, DNS, firewall, VPC: sab networking par based. Issues debug karne ke liye yeh must-have skill hai."}, {"t": "Networking: Deep Guide", "u": "02-networking/guide.html", "x": "Deployment, load balancer, DNS, firewall, VPC: sab networking par based. Issues debug karne ke liye yeh must-have skill hai. **Problem it solves:** Applications, users, databases and cloud services are on different machines. Networking is the set of rules (protocols) and devices that lets them find each other and exchange data reliably and securely. Most production incidents (\"site is down\", \"timeout\", \"cannot connect to DB\") are network problems until proven otherwise."}, {"t": "OSI, TCP/IP & How the Web Works - Networking", "u": "02-networking/osi-tcpip.html", "x": "Data layers se hokar jaata hai. Interview ka sabse common sawal: 'Browser mein URL daalne par kya hota hai?' dig +short example.com # 1. DNS -> IP traceroute example.com # 2. path of routers curl -v https://example.com 2>&1 | head -40 # 3. TCP, TLS, HTTP details curl -w 'dns:%{time_namelookup} connect:%{time_connect} tls:%{time_appconnect} total:%{time_total}\\n' -o /dev/null -s https://example.com"}, {"t": "IP Addressing & Subnetting (CIDR) - Networking", "u": "02-networking/ip-subnetting.html", "x": "VPC design karne ke liye CIDR aana chahiye. /24 = 256 addresses, /16 = 65,536. Private ranges: 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16. ipcalc 10.0.1.0/24 python3 -c \"import ipaddress as i; n=i.ip_network('10.0.0.0/16'); print([str(s) for s in list(n.subnets(new_prefix=24))[:4]])\" VPC 10.0.0.0/16 public 10.0.1.0/24, 10.0.2.0/24 (ALB, NAT) private 10.0.11.0/24, 10.0.12.0/24 (app) database 10.0.21.0/24, 10.0.22.0/24 (RDS)"}, {"t": "DNS Deep Dive - Networking", "u": "02-networking/dns.html", "x": "DNS name ko IP mein badalta hai. Aadhe production outages DNS ki wajah se hote hain. Records, TTL aur resolution flow samjho. dig example.com A +short dig example.com MX; dig example.com TXT dig +trace example.com dig @1.1.1.1 example.com # bypass local resolver nslookup -type=NS example.com resource \"aws_route53_record\" \"app\" { zone_id = var.zone_id name = \"app.example.com\" type = \"A\" set_identifier = \"blue\" weighted_routing_policy { weight = 90 } alias { name = aws_lb.blue.dns_name zone_id = aws_lb.blue.zone_id evaluate_target_health = true } }"}, {"t": "HTTP, HTTPS & TLS/SSL - Networking", "u": "02-networking/http-tls.html", "x": "Web ki language HTTP hai. HTTPS = HTTP + TLS encryption. Certificates, status codes aur headers debugging mein roz kaam aate hain. curl -I https://example.com curl -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"a\":1}' https://api/x -v openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com </dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -dates -issuer echo | openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate sudo certbot --nginx -d example.com -d www.example.com sudo certbot renew --dry-run"}, {"t": "Load Balancers, Reverse Proxy & Firewalls - Networking", "u": "02-networking/load-balancing-proxy.html", "x": "Traffic ko multiple servers mein baantna (LB), backend ko chhupana (reverse proxy), aur security rules (firewall/security groups). upstream backend { least_conn; server 10.0.11.10:3000 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=10s; server 10.0.11.11:3000; } server { listen 80; location / { proxy_pass http://backend; proxy_set_header Host $host; proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr; } } SG : stateful, allow rules only, attached to ENI/instance NACL : stateless, allow+deny, attached to subnet, evaluated in order"}, {"t": "Network Troubleshooting Playbook - Networking", "u": "02-networking/net-troubleshooting.html", "x": "Systematic approach: kaha tak packet ja raha hai? Layer by layer test karo. HOST=example.com; PORT=443 dig +short $HOST nc -zv -w3 $HOST $PORT mtr -rwc 10 $HOST sudo tcpdump -i any host $HOST and port $PORT -c 20 -nn 1 Route table has route (IGW/NAT)? 2 SG inbound/outbound allows port? 3 NACL allows both directions (ephemeral ports 1024-65535)? 4 Instance OS firewall/app listening on 0.0.0.0? Use: VPC Reachability Analyzer"}, {"t": "Git & Version Control", "u": "03-git/index.html", "x": "Code, infra, configs, dashboards: sab Git mein. GitOps ka base bhi Git hai."}, {"t": "Git & Version Control: Deep Guide", "u": "03-git/guide.html", "x": "Code, infra, configs, dashboards: sab Git mein. GitOps ka base bhi Git hai. **Problem it solves:** Many people change the same code, configs and infrastructure files. Without version control you get \"final_v2_new_FINAL.zip\", overwritten work, no history and no way to undo a bad change. **Git** records every change as a commit, lets people work in parallel on branches, and merges the work safely."}, {"t": "Git Basics & Workflow - Git & Version Control", "u": "03-git/git-basics.html", "x": "Git distributed version control hai. Har developer ke paas poori history hoti hai. Working dir -> staging -> local repo -> remote repo. git init myapp && cd myapp echo \"# myapp\" > README.md git add . && git commit -m \"feat: initial commit\" git remote add origin git@github.com:user/myapp.git git push -u origin main git status; git log --oneline --graph --all; git diff; git diff --staged node_modules/ .env *.log .terraform/ *.tfstate*"}, {"t": "Branching, Merge, Rebase & Conflicts - Git & Version Control", "u": "03-git/branching-merging.html", "x": "Branch se parallel kaam hota hai. Merge history preserve karta hai, rebase linear history banata hai. Conflict resolve karna seekho. git switch -c feature/login # ...work... git add -A && git commit -m \"feat(login): add form\" git fetch origin && git rebase origin/main git push -u origin feature/login # open PR git merge --no-ff feature/login # merge with merge commit git merge main # CONFLICT in app.js -> edit markers <<<<<<< ======= >>>>>>> git add app.js && git commit # or abort: git merge --abort git stash push -m wip; git stash pop git cherry-pick <sha> git bisect start; git bisect bad; gi"}, {"t": "Branching Strategies & PR Process - Git & Version Control", "u": "03-git/branching-strategies.html", "x": "Team mein kaunsa branching model? GitFlow, GitHub Flow, ya trunk-based. Modern DevOps teams trunk-based + feature flags prefer karte hain. Require PR + 1-2 approvals Require status checks (CI) to pass Require branches up to date Dismiss stale approvals; require CODEOWNERS review Block force-push and deletion # .github/CODEOWNERS /infra/ @org/platform-team *.tf @org/platform-team /app/ @org/backend feat: add login -> minor (1.1.0) fix: null pointer -> patch (1.0.1) feat!: drop v1 API -> major (2.0.0) git tag -a v1.2.0 -m \"release\" && git push --tags"}, {"t": "Git Hooks, Secrets & Advanced Git - Git & Version Control", "u": "03-git/git-hooks-security.html", "x": "Commit se pehle checks (pre-commit), secret scanning, signed commits, monorepo aur history cleanup. repos: - repo: https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks rev: v8.18.4 hooks: [{id: gitleaks}] - repo: https://github.com/antonbabenko/pre-commit-terraform rev: v1.92.0 hooks: [{id: terraform_fmt}, {id: terraform_validate}] - repo: https://github.com/pre-commit/pre-commit-hooks rev: v4.6.0 hooks: [{id: check-yaml}, {id: end-of-file-fixer}] git config --global commit.gpgsign true git config --global gpg.format ssh git config --global user.signingkey ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub git submodule add https://githu"}, {"t": "Python & Automation", "u": "04-python-automation/index.html", "x": "Bash chhoti tasks ke liye; Python bade automation, AWS SDK, APIs aur tools ke liye. DevOps mein Python sabse useful language hai."}, {"t": "Python & Automation: Deep Guide", "u": "04-python-automation/guide.html", "x": "Bash chhoti tasks ke liye; Python bade automation, AWS SDK, APIs aur tools ke liye. DevOps mein Python sabse useful language hai. **Problem it solves:** Repeating tasks by hand (creating servers, rotating keys, checking health, cleaning resources, calling APIs) is slow and error-prone. **Python (and Go)** let you automate these tasks with real programming features: data structures, error handling, libraries for AWS, APIs and files."}, {"t": "Python Basics for DevOps - Python & Automation", "u": "04-python-automation/python-basics.html", "x": "Variables, lists, dicts, functions, files, virtualenv. JSON/YAML parsing DevOps ka daily kaam hai. import json, yaml with open(\"config.yaml\") as f: cfg = yaml.safe_load(f) servers = cfg[\"servers\"] report = {s[\"name\"]: s[\"port\"] for s in servers if s.get(\"enabled\")} print(json.dumps(report, indent=2)) def read_file(path): try: with open(path) as f: return f.read() except FileNotFoundError: print(f\"missing: {path}\") return None"}, {"t": "AWS Automation with boto3 - Python & Automation", "u": "04-python-automation/boto3-aws-automation.html", "x": "boto3 se AWS resources programmatically manage karo: cost saving scripts, backups, audits. import boto3 ec2 = boto3.client(\"ec2\", region_name=\"ap-south-1\") resp = ec2.describe_instances(Filters=[ {\"Name\": \"tag:Env\", \"Values\": [\"dev\"]}, {\"Name\": \"instance-state-name\", \"Values\": [\"running\"]}]) ids = [i[\"InstanceId\"] for r in resp[\"Reservations\"] for i in r[\"Instances\"]] if ids: ec2.stop_instances(InstanceIds=ids) print(\"stopped\", ids) s3 = boto3.client(\"s3\") for b in s3.list_buckets()[\"Buckets\"]: try: st = s3.get_public_access_block(Bucket=b[\"Name\"])[\"PublicAccessBlockConfiguration\"] if not all("}, {"t": "APIs, CLI Tools & Monitoring Scripts - Python & Automation", "u": "04-python-automation/apis-cli-tools.html", "x": "REST APIs se baat karna (requests), CLI tool banana (argparse), aur Slack alerts bhejna. import os, sys, requests URLS = [\"https://example.com/health\", \"https://api.example.com/health\"] hook = os.environ[\"SLACK_WEBHOOK\"] bad = [] for u in URLS: try: r = requests.get(u, timeout=5) if r.status_code != 200: bad.append(f\"{u} -> {r.status_code}\") except requests.RequestException as e: bad.append(f\"{u} -> {e}\") if bad: requests.post(hook, json={\"text\": \"ALERT:\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(bad)}) sys.exit(1) import argparse p = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"Deploy helper\") p.add_argument(\"env\", choices=[\"de"}, {"t": "Go for DevOps & Kubernetes Operators (Intro) - Python & Automation", "u": "04-python-automation/go-and-operators.html", "x": "Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform, Prometheus sab Go mein bane hain. Go static binary deta hai, isliye tools ke liye best. Operators custom controllers hote hain. package main import (\"fmt\";\"net/http\";\"os\";\"time\") func main() { c := http.Client{Timeout: 5 * time.Second} r, err := c.Get(os.Args[1]) if err != nil || r.StatusCode != 200 { fmt.Println(\"DOWN\"); os.Exit(1) } fmt.Println(\"UP\") } // go build -o check && ./check https://example.com (cross-compile: GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build) for { desired := getCR() actual := getDeployment() if actual != desired { apply(desired) } sleep() }"}, {"t": "Docker & Containers", "u": "05-docker/index.html", "x": "'Mere machine pe chal raha hai' ka solution. App + dependencies ek image mein pack hote hain aur har jagah same chalte hain."}, {"t": "Docker & Containers: Deep Guide", "u": "05-docker/guide.html", "x": "'Mere machine pe chal raha hai' ka solution. App + dependencies ek image mein pack hote hain aur har jagah same chalte hain. **Problem it solves:** \"Mere laptop pe chal raha hai, server pe nahi chal raha.\" Without Docker, every machine has a different OS, Node/Python version and library set, so the same app behaves differently. With Docker, the application **and** its dependencies are packaged into one image that runs the same way on a laptop, a CI server and production."}, {"t": "Docker Basics: Images & Containers - Docker & Containers", "u": "05-docker/docker-basics.html", "x": "Image = read-only template. Container = running instance. VM poora OS chalata hai, container host kernel share karta hai isliye halka aur fast hai. docker pull nginx:1.27 docker run -d --name web -p 8080:80 nginx:1.27 docker ps; docker ps -a docker logs -f web docker exec -it web sh docker stop web && docker rm web docker images; docker rmi nginx:1.27 docker system prune -af # cleanup unused docker run -d --name api -p 3000:3000 \\ -e NODE_ENV=production --memory=256m --cpus=0.5 \\ --restart unless-stopped myapp:1.0"}, {"t": "Dockerfile & Image Optimization - Docker & Containers", "u": "05-docker/dockerfile.html", "x": "Dockerfile image ka recipe hai. Layer order, cache, multi-stage builds, non-root user aur chhoti image production ke liye zaroori hain. FROM node:20-alpine AS build WORKDIR /app COPY package*.json ./ # copy deps first -> cache friendly RUN npm ci COPY . . RUN npm run build FROM node:20-alpine WORKDIR /app ENV NODE_ENV=production COPY --from=build /app/dist ./dist COPY --from=build /app/node_modules ./node_modules USER node EXPOSE 3000 HEALTHCHECK CMD wget -qO- localhost:3000/health || exit 1 CMD [\"node\",\"dist/server.js\"] node_modules .git .env *.log FROM python:3.12-slim WORKDIR /app COPY requ"}, {"t": "Docker Networking & Volumes - Docker & Containers", "u": "05-docker/docker-networking-volumes.html", "x": "Containers ko aapas mein baat karwana (networks) aur data persist karna (volumes). Container delete hone par uska data chala jata hai, isliye volumes. docker network create appnet docker volume create pgdata docker run -d --name db --network appnet -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data postgres:16 docker run -d --name api --network appnet -e DB_HOST=db -p 3000:3000 myapp:1.0 docker network inspect appnet; docker volume ls docker run --rm -v pgdata:/data -v $PWD:/backup alpine tar czf /backup/pgdata.tgz -C /data ."}, {"t": "Docker Compose (Multi-container Apps) - Docker & Containers", "u": "05-docker/docker-compose.html", "x": "Ek YAML mein pura stack: web + api + db + redis. Local development aur small deployments ke liye best. services: web: image: nginx:1.27 ports: [\"80:80\"] volumes: [\"./nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf:ro\"] depends_on: [api] api: build: ./api environment: DB_URL: postgres://app:secret@db:5432/app REDIS: redis:6379 depends_on: db: { condition: service_healthy } db: image: postgres:16 environment: { POSTGRES_USER: app, POSTGRES_PASSWORD: secret, POSTGRES_DB: app } volumes: [pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data] healthcheck: test: [\"CMD-SHELL\",\"pg_isready -U app\"] interval: 5s redis: image: redi"}, {"t": "Container Security & Internals - Docker & Containers", "u": "05-docker/docker-security-internals.html", "x": "Container = Linux process + namespaces + cgroups. Security: non-root, minimal images, scanning, read-only FS, dropped capabilities, signing. docker run -d --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\ --cap-drop ALL --security-opt no-new-privileges \\ --user 10001 --pids-limit 200 myapp:1.0 trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 myapp:1.0 cosign sign --key cosign.key registry/myapp:1.0 syft myapp:1.0 -o spdx-json > sbom.json sudo unshare --pid --fork --mount-proc bash # own PID namespace ps aux # only shows processes in this namespace"}, {"t": "CI/CD", "u": "06-cicd/index.html", "x": "Code push se production tak sab kuch automatic: build, test, scan, deploy. DevOps ka heart."}, {"t": "CI/CD: Deep Guide", "u": "06-cicd/guide.html", "x": "Code push se production tak sab kuch automatic: build, test, scan, deploy. DevOps ka heart. **Problem it solves:** Manual releases are slow, risky and inconsistent: someone builds on a laptop, forgets to run tests, copies files to a server by hand, and a bug reaches users. **CI/CD** (Continuous Integration / Continuous Delivery or Deployment) automates the path from a code commit to a running application, with tests and checks at every step."}, {"t": "CI/CD Concepts & Pipeline Design - CI/CD", "u": "06-cicd/cicd-concepts.html", "x": "CI: har commit par build+test. Continuous Delivery: hamesha deployable, release manual approval se. Continuous Deployment: har pass hua change automatic prod mein. 1. Fast feedback (<10 min for CI) 2. Same artifact promoted across envs (build once, deploy many) 3. Immutable, versioned artifacts (image tag = git sha) 4. Config/secrets injected per env, not baked in 5. Automated rollback & smoke tests 6. Everything as code (Jenkinsfile / workflow yaml)"}, {"t": "GitHub Actions - CI/CD", "u": "06-cicd/github-actions.html", "x": "Repo ke andar `.github/workflows/*.yml`. Events (push/PR) par jobs run hote hain runners par. Free tier generous hai. name: ci on: push: { branches: [main] } pull_request: jobs: test: runs-on: ubuntu-latest strategy: matrix: { node: [18, 20] } steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - uses: actions/setup-node@v4 with: { node-version: \"${{ matrix.node }}\", cache: npm } - run: npm ci - run: npm test -- --coverage - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4 with: { name: coverage, path: coverage/ } name: release on: { push: { tags: ['v*'] } } permissions: { contents: read, packages: write } jobs: image: runs-o"}, {"t": "Jenkins - CI/CD", "u": "06-cicd/jenkins.html", "x": "Sabse popular self-hosted CI server. Jenkinsfile (pipeline as code), agents, plugins. Enterprises mein abhi bhi bahut use hota hai. pipeline { agent any environment { IMAGE = \"myrepo/app:${env.GIT_COMMIT[0..6]}\" } options { timeout(time: 30, unit: 'MINUTES'); disableConcurrentBuilds() } stages { stage('Test') { steps { sh 'npm ci && npm test' } } stage('Build') { steps { sh 'docker build -t $IMAGE .' } } stage('Push') { steps { withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId:'dockerhub', usernameVariable:'U', passwordVariable:'P')]) { sh 'echo $P | docker login -u $U --password-stdin && docker"}, {"t": "GitOps & ArgoCD - CI/CD", "u": "06-cicd/gitops-argocd.html", "x": "Git = single source of truth for cluster state. ArgoCD Git repo watch karta hai aur cluster ko usse sync karta hai (pull-based CD). Drift auto-heal hota hai. apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: Application metadata: { name: myapp, namespace: argocd } spec: project: default source: { repoURL: https://github.com/acme/gitops, targetRevision: main, path: charts/myapp } destination: { server: https://kubernetes.default.svc, namespace: prod } syncPolicy: { automated: { prune: true, selfHeal: true } } git revert <bad-commit> && git push # ArgoCD syncs the previous state argocd app history myapp; a"}, {"t": "Deployment Strategies & Pipeline Security - CI/CD", "u": "06-cicd/deployment-strategies.html", "x": "Zero-downtime release techniques: rolling, blue-green, canary. Saath mein pipeline security (SAST, SCA, secrets, signing). apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: Rollout metadata: { name: myapp } spec: replicas: 5 selector: { matchLabels: { app: myapp } } template: metadata: { labels: { app: myapp } } spec: { containers: [{ name: myapp, image: myapp:v2 }] } strategy: canary: steps: - setWeight: 10 - pause: { duration: 5m } - setWeight: 50 - pause: { duration: 10m } 1 gitleaks (secrets) 2 SonarQube/Semgrep (SAST) 3 Trivy fs/deps (SCA) 4 Trivy image scan 5 cosign sign + SBOM 6 DAST (OWASP ZAP) o"}, {"t": "Kubernetes", "u": "07-kubernetes/index.html", "x": "Container orchestrator: scaling, self-healing, rolling updates, service discovery. Job market mein sabse zyada demand."}, {"t": "Kubernetes: Deep Guide", "u": "07-kubernetes/guide.html", "x": "Container orchestrator: scaling, self-healing, rolling updates, service discovery. Job market mein sabse zyada demand. **Problem it solves:** Docker runs containers on one machine. In production you need dozens or hundreds of containers across many servers. Who restarts a crashed container? Who spreads load? Who does rolling updates without downtime? **Kubernetes (K8s)** is the container orchestrator that does this automatically: you declare the **desired state** (\"run 3 copies of this image\") and Kubernetes keeps reality equal to it."}, {"t": "Architecture & Local Setup - Kubernetes", "u": "07-kubernetes/k8s-architecture.html", "x": "Cluster = Control Plane (brain) + Worker Nodes (muscle). Aap desired state YAML dete ho; Kubernetes actual state ko wahi banata hai. kubectl get ns; kubectl get pods -A kubectl run nginx --image=nginx:1.27 kubectl get pods -o wide kubectl describe pod nginx # events = first place to debug kubectl logs nginx; kubectl exec -it nginx -- sh kubectl delete pod nginx kubectl api-resources; kubectl explain deployment.spec"}, {"t": "Pods, ReplicaSets & Deployments - Kubernetes", "u": "07-kubernetes/pods-deployments.html", "x": "Pod = smallest unit (1+ containers sharing network/storage). Deployment pods ko manage karta hai: replicas, rolling update, rollback. apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: { name: web } spec: replicas: 3 selector: { matchLabels: { app: web } } strategy: { type: RollingUpdate, rollingUpdate: { maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0 } } template: metadata: { labels: { app: web } } spec: containers: - name: web image: nginx:1.27 ports: [{ containerPort: 80 }] resources: requests: { cpu: 100m, memory: 128Mi } limits: { cpu: 500m, memory: 256Mi } readinessProbe: { httpGet: { path: /, port: 80 }, i"}, {"t": "Services, Ingress & Networking - Kubernetes", "u": "07-kubernetes/services-ingress.html", "x": "Pod IPs change hote hain. Service stable virtual IP + DNS deta hai. Ingress HTTP routing (host/path) aur TLS handle karta hai. apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: { name: web } spec: selector: { app: web } ports: [{ port: 80, targetPort: 80 }] --- apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: name: web annotations: { cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt } spec: ingressClassName: nginx tls: [{ hosts: [app.example.com], secretName: app-tls }] rules: - host: app.example.com http: paths: - { path: /, pathType: Prefix, backend: { service: { name: web, port: { number: 80 } } } }"}, {"t": "ConfigMaps, Secrets & Persistent Storage - Kubernetes", "u": "07-kubernetes/config-storage.html", "x": "Config ko image se alag rakho (ConfigMap), sensitive data Secret mein, aur database ke liye PV/PVC + StatefulSet. kubectl create configmap app-config --from-literal=LOG_LEVEL=info kubectl create secret generic db-secret --from-literal=password='S3cret!' # use in pod envFrom: - configMapRef: { name: app-config } env: - name: DB_PASSWORD valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: db-secret, key: password } } apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: StatefulSet metadata: { name: pg } spec: serviceName: pg replicas: 1 selector: { matchLabels: { app: pg } } template: metadata: { labels: { app: pg } } spec: containers: - "}, {"t": "Scaling, Scheduling & Resource Management - Kubernetes", "u": "07-kubernetes/scaling-scheduling.html", "x": "HPA pods scale karta hai, cluster autoscaler/Karpenter nodes. Scheduling controls: affinity, taints, topology spread, PDB. kubectl autoscale deploy web --cpu-percent=70 --min=2 --max=10 kubectl get hpa -w topologySpreadConstraints: - maxSkew: 1 topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule labelSelector: { matchLabels: { app: web } } --- apiVersion: policy/v1 kind: PodDisruptionBudget metadata: { name: web } spec: { minAvailable: 2, selector: { matchLabels: { app: web } } } kubectl taint nodes gpu1 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule # pod toleration: key dedicated, value gpu,"}, {"t": "Helm, RBAC & Cluster Security - Kubernetes", "u": "07-kubernetes/helm-rbac-security.html", "x": "Helm K8s package manager (templates + values). RBAC access control. Policies (Kyverno/OPA) aur Pod Security se hardening. helm create myapp helm install web ./myapp -f values-prod.yaml -n prod --create-namespace helm upgrade web ./myapp --set image.tag=v2 --atomic helm rollback web 1; helm list -A; helm template ./myapp # render only apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: Role metadata: { name: pod-reader, namespace: dev } rules: [{ apiGroups: [\"\"], resources: [pods, pods/log], verbs: [get, list, watch] }] --- kind: RoleBinding apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 metadata: { name:"}, {"t": "Kubernetes Troubleshooting Playbook - Kubernetes", "u": "07-kubernetes/k8s-troubleshooting.html", "x": "Interview aur production dono mein: pod kyun fail hua? Systematic flow follow karo. kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp -n prod kubectl describe pod <p>; kubectl logs <p> -c <container> --previous kubectl top pod; kubectl top node kubectl debug -it <pod> --image=busybox --target=<container> kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=nicolaka/netshoot -- bash # nslookup, curl, dig kubectl get endpoints svc; kubectl port-forward svc/web 8080:80 journalctl -u kubelet -f # on node 137 = SIGKILL (OOMKilled), 143 = SIGTERM, 1 = app error, 126/127 = command not executable/not found"}, {"t": "Cloud (AWS)", "u": "08-aws-cloud/index.html", "x": "Ek cloud deeply seekho (AWS). Concepts Azure/GCP mein bhi same hain. Free tier par practice karo aur budget alert zaroor lagao."}, {"t": "Cloud (AWS): Deep Guide", "u": "08-aws-cloud/guide.html", "x": "Ek cloud deeply seekho (AWS). Concepts Azure/GCP mein bhi same hain. Free tier par practice karo aur budget alert zaroor lagao. **Problem it solves:** Buying, racking and maintaining servers takes weeks and large upfront cost, and you must provision for peak load. **Cloud (AWS = Amazon Web Services)** gives you computing, storage, databases and networking on demand, paid per use, in minutes, across many data centres."}, {"t": "AWS Basics, Account Setup & IAM - Cloud (AWS)", "u": "08-aws-cloud/aws-iam-basics.html", "x": "Region = geographic area, AZ = isolated data center in region. IAM decides WHO can do WHAT. Root account use mat karo, MFA lagao. { \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": [{ \"Effect\": \"Allow\", \"Action\": [\"s3:GetObject\",\"s3:PutObject\"], \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-bucket/*\" }] } 1 Enable MFA on root; don't create root access keys 2 Create admin IAM Identity Center user; use SSO 3 Budget alert at $5/$10 (Billing -> Budgets) 4 Enable CloudTrail; set region restrictions via SCP later"}, {"t": "VPC Networking - Cloud (AWS)", "u": "08-aws-cloud/aws-vpc.html", "x": "VPC tumhara private network hai AWS mein. Subnets, route tables, IGW, NAT, SG, NACL: har production design ka base. aws ec2 create-vpc --cidr-block 10.0.0.0/16 aws ec2 create-subnet --vpc-id vpc-123 --cidr-block 10.0.1.0/24 --availability-zone ap-south-1a aws ec2 create-internet-gateway; aws ec2 attach-internet-gateway --vpc-id vpc-123 --internet-gateway-id igw-123 aws ec2 create-route --route-table-id rtb-123 --destination-cidr-block 0.0.0.0/0 --gateway-id igw-123 Use VPC Reachability Analyzer; check: route table -> SG -> NACL -> OS firewall -> app bind address"}, {"t": "EC2, Auto Scaling & Load Balancing - Cloud (AWS)", "u": "08-aws-cloud/aws-compute-ec2.html", "x": "EC2 virtual servers hain. ASG instances ko scale/replace karta hai, ALB traffic distribute karta hai. Classic 3-tier HA pattern. aws ec2 run-instances --image-id ami-xxxx --instance-type t3.micro \\ --subnet-id subnet-123 --security-group-ids sg-123 --iam-instance-profile Name=app-role \\ --user-data file://userdata.sh --tag-specifications 'ResourceType=instance,Tags=[{Key=Env,Value=dev}]' # userdata.sh #!/bin/bash apt update && apt install -y nginx systemctl enable --now nginx aws autoscaling put-scaling-policy --auto-scaling-group-name web-asg --policy-name cpu50 \\ --policy-type TargetTracking"}, {"t": "S3, EBS, RDS & DynamoDB - Cloud (AWS)", "u": "08-aws-cloud/aws-storage-db.html", "x": "S3 object storage (static sites, backups, logs). RDS managed relational DB. DynamoDB serverless NoSQL. aws s3 mb s3://my-unique-bucket-123 aws s3 sync ./dist s3://my-unique-bucket-123 --delete aws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket my-unique-bucket-123 --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled aws s3 presign s3://my-unique-bucket-123/file.pdf --expires-in 600 {\"Rules\":[{\"ID\":\"archive\",\"Status\":\"Enabled\",\"Filter\":{\"Prefix\":\"logs/\"}, \"Transitions\":[{\"Days\":30,\"StorageClass\":\"STANDARD_IA\"},{\"Days\":90,\"StorageClass\":\"GLACIER\"}], \"Expiration\":{\"Days\":365}}]} aws rds create-db-snapshot --db-instan"}, {"t": "Lambda, ECS/EKS, CloudWatch & Cost - Cloud (AWS)", "u": "08-aws-cloud/aws-serverless-monitoring.html", "x": "Serverless (Lambda), containers (ECS Fargate/EKS), monitoring (CloudWatch/CloudTrail), aur cost optimization (FinOps). Resources: Fn: Type: AWS::Serverless::Function Properties: Runtime: python3.12 Handler: app.handler Timeout: 30 Events: Nightly: { Type: Schedule, Properties: { Schedule: cron(0 20 * * ? *) } } aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm --alarm-name high-cpu --namespace AWS/EC2 --metric-name CPUUtilization \\ --statistic Average --period 300 --threshold 80 --comparison-operator GreaterThanThreshold --evaluation-periods 2 \\ --alarm-actions arn:aws:sns:ap-south-1:123456789012:ops-alerts Rig"}, {"t": "Infrastructure as Code: Terraform", "u": "09-terraform/index.html", "x": "Infra ko code ki tarah likho: repeatable, reviewable, version-controlled. Click-ops (console) se chhutkara."}, {"t": "Infrastructure as Code: Terraform: Deep Guide", "u": "09-terraform/guide.html", "x": "Infra ko code ki tarah likho: repeatable, reviewable, version-controlled. Click-ops (console) se chhutkara. **Problem it solves:** Creating cloud infrastructure by clicking in a console is slow, undocumented and impossible to reproduce exactly. **Terraform** (Infrastructure as Code, IaC) lets you describe infrastructure in files, review it like code, and create, change or destroy it repeatably with one workflow."}, {"t": "Terraform Basics & Workflow - Infrastructure as Code: Terraform", "u": "09-terraform/terraform-basics.html", "x": "Declarative HCL likhte ho; Terraform current state (tfstate) se compare karke plan banata hai aur apply karta hai. Workflow: init -> plan -> apply -> destroy. terraform { required_providers { aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\", version = \"~> 5.0\" } } } provider \"aws\" { region = \"ap-south-1\" } variable \"env\" { default = \"dev\" } resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"logs\" { bucket = \"acme-logs-${var.env}-12345\" tags = { Env = var.env } } data \"aws_ami\" \"ubuntu\" { most_recent = true owners = [\"099720109477\"] filter { name = \"name\", values = [\"ubuntu/images/hvm-ssd/ubuntu-jammy-22.04-amd64-server-*\"] } } resour"}, {"t": "Remote State, Modules & Variables - Infrastructure as Code: Terraform", "u": "09-terraform/terraform-state-modules.html", "x": "Team mein kaam karne ke liye remote state + locking. Modules reuse ke liye. Variables, locals, for_each, dynamic blocks. terraform { backend \"s3\" { bucket = \"acme-tfstate\" key = \"prod/network.tfstate\" region = \"ap-south-1\" dynamodb_table = \"tf-locks\" encrypt = true } } modules/vpc/{main.tf,variables.tf,outputs.tf} module \"vpc\" { source = \"../../modules/vpc\" name = \"prod\" cidr = \"10.0.0.0/16\" azs = [\"ap-south-1a\", \"ap-south-1b\"] } # use output: module.vpc.private_subnet_ids variable \"buckets\" { default = [\"logs\", \"backups\"] } resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"b\" { for_each = toset(var.buckets) bucket ="}, {"t": "Real Project: VPC + EKS + RDS - Infrastructure as Code: Terraform", "u": "09-terraform/terraform-real-infra.html", "x": "Full production-style stack Terraform mein: network, cluster, database, IAM. Community modules reuse karo. module \"vpc\" { source = \"terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws\" version = \"~> 5.0\" name = \"prod\" cidr = \"10.0.0.0/16\" azs = [\"ap-south-1a\", \"ap-south-1b\", \"ap-south-1c\"] private_subnets = [\"10.0.1.0/24\", \"10.0.2.0/24\", \"10.0.3.0/24\"] public_subnets = [\"10.0.101.0/24\", \"10.0.102.0/24\", \"10.0.103.0/24\"] enable_nat_gateway = true single_nat_gateway = false } module \"eks\" { source = \"terraform-aws-modules/eks/aws\" version = \"~> 20.0\" cluster_name = \"prod\" cluster_version = \"1.30\" vpc_id = module.vpc."}, {"t": "Terraform in CI, Policy & Testing - Infrastructure as Code: Terraform", "u": "09-terraform/terraform-ci-policy.html", "x": "PR par plan comment, merge par apply. Checkov/tfsec/OPA se policy checks. Terratest/terraform test se testing. name: terraform on: { pull_request: { paths: ['infra/**'] } } permissions: { id-token: write, contents: read, pull-requests: write } jobs: plan: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3 - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4 with: { role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/tf-plan, aws-region: ap-south-1 } - run: terraform -chdir=infra/envs/prod init - run: terraform -chdir=infra/envs/prod fmt -check - run: checkov "}, {"t": "Configuration Management: Ansible", "u": "10-ansible/index.html", "x": "Servers ka setup, patching, app deploy: agentless, SSH par chalta hai, YAML playbooks. Terraform infra banata hai, Ansible usko configure karta hai."}, {"t": "Configuration Management: Ansible: Deep Guide", "u": "10-ansible/guide.html", "x": "Servers ka setup, patching, app deploy: agentless, SSH par chalta hai, YAML playbooks. Terraform infra banata hai, Ansible usko configure karta hai. **Problem it solves:** After servers exist, each one still needs packages, users, config files, patches and app deployments. Doing that by hand or with one-off scripts makes servers different from each other (configuration drift). **Ansible** describes the desired configuration in simple YAML and applies it to many servers over SSH, safely repeatable (idempotent), with no agent to install."}, {"t": "Ansible Basics: Inventory, Ad-hoc & Playbooks - Configuration Management: Ansible", "u": "10-ansible/ansible-basics.html", "x": "Control node se SSH karke managed nodes par tasks run hote hain. Modules idempotent hote hain: dobara chalao to same result. # inventory.ini [web] web1 ansible_host=10.0.1.10 web2 ansible_host=10.0.1.11 [db] db1 ansible_host=10.0.2.10 [all:vars] ansible_user=ubuntu ansible_ssh_private_key_file=~/.ssh/id_ed25519 ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping ansible web -i inventory.ini -m shell -a \"uptime\" ansible web -i inventory.ini -b -m apt -a \"name=nginx state=present\" - name: Setup web servers hosts: web become: true vars: { http_port: 80 } tasks: - name: Install nginx apt: { name: nginx, state: p"}, {"t": "Roles, Templates, Variables & Vault - Configuration Management: Ansible", "u": "10-ansible/ansible-roles-templates.html", "x": "Playbook bada ho jaye to roles mein todo. Jinja2 templates se config files generate karo. Vault se secrets encrypt karo. # templates/app.conf.j2 server { listen {{ http_port }}; server_name {{ domain }}; location / { proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:{{ app_port }}; } } # task - template: { src: app.conf.j2, dest: /etc/nginx/conf.d/app.conf, validate: 'nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/nginx.conf' } notify: reload nginx - apt: { name: \"{{ item }}\", state: present } loop: [git, curl, htop] when: ansible_os_family == \"Debian\" tags: [packages] # ansible-playbook site.yml --tags packages --limit web1 ansible-vault"}, {"t": "Real Use Cases: Hardening, Patching, Packer & AWX - Configuration Management: Ansible", "u": "10-ansible/ansible-real-usage.html", "x": "Production mein Ansible: server hardening, patching, AMI baking (Packer), testing (Molecule), aur AWX UI. - name: Disable root SSH and passwords lineinfile: { path: /etc/ssh/sshd_config, regexp: '^#?{{ item.k }}', line: '{{ item.k }} {{ item.v }}' } loop: [{ k: PermitRootLogin, v: 'no' }, { k: PasswordAuthentication, v: 'no' }] notify: reload ssh - name: Enable UFW ufw: { state: enabled, policy: deny } - ufw: { rule: allow, port: '22', proto: tcp } source \"amazon-ebs\" \"ubuntu\" { region = \"ap-south-1\"; instance_type = \"t3.micro\" source_ami_filter { filters = { name = \"ubuntu/images/*22.04-amd64"}, {"t": "Monitoring, Logging & Observability", "u": "11-monitoring-observability/index.html", "x": "Jo dikhta nahi usse fix nahi kar sakte. Metrics + Logs + Traces = observability. Alerts sahi hone chahiye, noisy nahi."}, {"t": "Monitoring, Logging & Observability: Deep Guide", "u": "11-monitoring-observability/guide.html", "x": "Jo dikhta nahi usse fix nahi kar sakte. Metrics + Logs + Traces = observability. Alerts sahi hone chahiye, noisy nahi. **Problem it solves:** Production systems fail in surprising ways. Without visibility you learn about problems from angry customers, and you cannot find the cause quickly. **Monitoring** tells you *that* something is wrong; **observability** (metrics + logs + traces) helps you understand *why*, even for problems you did not predict."}, {"t": "Observability Concepts & Golden Signals - Monitoring, Logging & Observability", "u": "11-monitoring-observability/observability-concepts.html", "x": "Monitoring 'kya toota' batati hai, observability 'kyun toota'. Three pillars: metrics (numbers), logs (events), traces (request path). Alert on SYMPTOMS (user pain: error rate, latency), not causes (CPU 80%). Every page must be actionable + have a runbook. Severity: page (wake up) vs ticket (next day) vs info."}, {"t": "Prometheus & PromQL - Monitoring, Logging & Observability", "u": "11-monitoring-observability/prometheus.html", "x": "Pull-based metrics system. Targets ko scrape karta hai, time-series DB mein store. PromQL query language. Kubernetes ka de-facto standard. global: { scrape_interval: 15s } scrape_configs: - job_name: node static_configs: [{ targets: ['localhost:9100'] }] - job_name: myapp metrics_path: /metrics static_configs: [{ targets: ['app:3000'] }] # CPU usage % 100 - avg by(instance)(rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode=\"idle\"}[5m])) * 100 # request rate and error ratio sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m])) sum(rate(http_requests_total{code=~\"5..\"}[5m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m])) # p95 latency histo"}, {"t": "Grafana Dashboards & Alertmanager - Monitoring, Logging & Observability", "u": "11-monitoring-observability/grafana-alertmanager.html", "x": "Grafana visualization, Alertmanager routing/grouping/silencing of alerts. Actionable alerts with runbooks. groups: - name: app rules: - alert: HighErrorRate expr: sum(rate(http_requests_total{code=~\"5..\"}[5m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m])) > 0.02 for: 5m labels: { severity: page } annotations: { summary: \"5xx > 2%\", runbook: \"https://wiki/runbooks/5xx\" } - alert: DiskFilling expr: predict_linear(node_filesystem_avail_bytes[6h], 24*3600) < 0 for: 30m labels: { severity: ticket } route: receiver: slack group_by: [alertname, cluster] routes: - matchers: [severity=\"page\"] receiver: pagerdu"}, {"t": "Centralized Logging (ELK & Loki) - Monitoring, Logging & Observability", "u": "11-monitoring-observability/logging-elk-loki.html", "x": "Logs har server par mat dekho, ek jagah collect karo. Structured JSON logs best hain. Loki lightweight (labels index), ELK full-text search. {namespace=\"prod\", app=\"api\"} |= \"error\" sum by(app)(rate({namespace=\"prod\"} |= \"error\" [5m])) {app=\"api\"} | json | level=\"error\" | line_format \"{{.msg}}\" [INPUT] Name tail Path /var/log/containers/*.log [FILTER] Name kubernetes [OUTPUT] Name es Host elasticsearch Index app-logs"}, {"t": "Tracing (OpenTelemetry) & SLOs - Monitoring, Logging & Observability", "u": "11-monitoring-observability/tracing-slo.html", "x": "Microservices mein ek request kai services se jaati hai; tracing se bottleneck milta hai. SLO aur error budget se reliability vs feature velocity balance hoti hai. pip install opentelemetry-distro opentelemetry-exporter-otlp opentelemetry-bootstrap -a install OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=orders OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel:4317 opentelemetry-instrument python app.py # SLI: good requests / total sum(rate(http_requests_total{code!~\"5..\"}[5m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m])) # multi-window burn rate for 99.9% SLO (sum(rate(http_requests_total{code=~\"5..\"}[1h])) / sum(rate(http_requests_tota"}, {"t": "DevSecOps & Security", "u": "12-devsecops/index.html", "x": "Security ko end mein nahi, shuru se (shift-left) add karo. Code, dependencies, images, infra aur runtime sab cover karo."}, {"t": "DevSecOps & Security: Deep Guide", "u": "12-devsecops/guide.html", "x": "Security ko end mein nahi, shuru se (shift-left) add karo. Code, dependencies, images, infra aur runtime sab cover karo. **Problem it solves:** If security checks happen only at the end (a penetration test before go-live), vulnerabilities are found late, fixes are expensive and releases are blocked. **DevSecOps** builds security into every step of the delivery pipeline (\"shift left\") and into runtime, so problems are caught early, automatically and continuously."}, {"t": "Security Fundamentals & Threat Thinking - DevSecOps & Security", "u": "12-devsecops/security-fundamentals.html", "x": "CIA triad (Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability), least privilege, defense in depth. OWASP Top 10 awareness aur common DevOps mistakes. 1 Secrets in Git/images/env logs 2 Over-permissive IAM (*:*) 3 Public S3/DB/SG 0.0.0.0/0 on 22/3306 4 Running containers as root 5 No patching/old base images 6 No audit logs / MFA"}, {"t": "Secrets Management (Vault, Secrets Manager) - DevSecOps & Security", "u": "12-devsecops/secrets-management.html", "x": "Passwords, tokens, keys ko Git ya env files mein mat rakho. Secret manager use karo: rotation, audit, dynamic short-lived credentials. vault kv put secret/myapp db_password=S3cret vault kv get -field=db_password secret/myapp cat > app.hcl <<EOF path \"secret/data/myapp\" { capabilities = [\"read\"] } EOF vault policy write myapp app.hcl apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1beta1 kind: ExternalSecret metadata: { name: db } spec: refreshInterval: 1h secretStoreRef: { name: aws, kind: ClusterSecretStore } target: { name: db-secret } data: - { secretKey: password, remoteRef: { key: prod/db, property: pas"}, {"t": "Scanning: SAST, SCA, DAST, Image & IaC - DevSecOps & Security", "u": "12-devsecops/pipeline-security-scanning.html", "x": "Har stage ke liye scanner: code (SAST), dependencies (SCA), running app (DAST), images (Trivy), Terraform (Checkov). CI mein gate lagao. security: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 with: { fetch-depth: 0 } - uses: gitleaks/gitleaks-action@v2 - uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@master with: { scan-type: fs, severity: 'HIGH,CRITICAL', exit-code: '1', ignore-unfixed: true } - run: pip install checkov && checkov -d infra --quiet - run: docker run --rm -v $PWD:/zap/wrk owasp/zap2docker-stable zap-baseline.py -t https://staging.example.com trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH -"}, {"t": "Cloud, Network & Kubernetes Runtime Security - DevSecOps & Security", "u": "12-devsecops/cloud-k8s-security.html", "x": "IAM hygiene, network segmentation, encryption, K8s policies aur runtime detection (Falco). Compliance aur incident response. securityContext: runAsNonRoot: true seccompProfile: { type: RuntimeDefault } containers: - name: app securityContext: allowPrivilegeEscalation: false readOnlyRootFilesystem: true capabilities: { drop: [ALL] } kubectl label ns prod pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted ServiceAccount annotation eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn -> pod gets scoped AWS creds, no node-wide role Detect -> Contain (isolate SG/pod, revoke creds) -> Eradicate -> Recover -> Post-incident review "}, {"t": "Web Servers, Databases & Messaging", "u": "13-webservers-databases/index.html", "x": "Production apps ke saath yeh components hamesha hote hain: Nginx, DB, cache, queue. DevOps engineer ko inhe deploy, tune aur troubleshoot karna aana chahiye."}, {"t": "Web Servers, Databases & Messaging: Deep Guide", "u": "13-webservers-databases/guide.html", "x": "Production apps ke saath yeh components hamesha hote hain: Nginx, DB, cache, queue. DevOps engineer ko inhe deploy, tune aur troubleshoot karna aana chahiye. **Problem it solves:** An application alone is not a production system. It needs a fast front door (web server / reverse proxy), durable storage (database), a way to avoid hitting the database for every request (cache) and a way to handle slow or bursty work without blocking users (message queues). DevOps engineers deploy, tune, secure and troubleshoot all of these."}, {"t": "Nginx: Web Server & Reverse Proxy - Web Servers, Databases & Messaging", "u": "13-webservers-databases/nginx.html", "x": "Nginx static files serve karta hai, reverse proxy/load balancer aur SSL terminator ka kaam karta hai. Event-driven, bahut fast. limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=api:10m rate=10r/s; server { listen 443 ssl http2; server_name app.example.com; ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/app.example.com/fullchain.pem; ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/app.example.com/privkey.pem; gzip on; gzip_types text/css application/json application/javascript; location / { root /var/www/html; try_files $uri /index.html; } # SPA location /api/ { limit_req zone=api burst=20 nodelay; proxy_pass http://1"}, {"t": "Relational Databases (PostgreSQL/MySQL) Operations - Web Servers, Databases & Messaging", "u": "13-webservers-databases/sql-databases.html", "x": "DevOps ko DB admin nahi banna, par backup, restore, replication, connection pooling, slow query basics aani chahiye. pg_dump -h host -U app -Fc appdb > appdb.dump pg_restore -h host -U app -d appdb_new --clean appdb.dump # MySQL: mysqldump --single-transaction db > db.sql SELECT query, calls, mean_exec_time FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY mean_exec_time DESC LIMIT 5; EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 42; CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user ON orders(user_id); SELECT count(*), state FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY state; SELECT pg_terminate_backend(pid) FROM pg_stat_act"}, {"t": "Redis & Caching - Web Servers, Databases & Messaging", "u": "13-webservers-databases/redis-cache.html", "x": "In-memory key-value store: cache, sessions, rate limits, queues. Latency kam aur DB load kam. SET user:1 '{\"name\":\"a\"}' EX 300 GET user:1; TTL user:1 INCR rate:ip:1.2.3.4; EXPIRE rate:ip:1.2.3.4 60 LPUSH queue job1; BRPOP queue 0 INFO memory; MONITOR # careful in prod maxmemory 2gb maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru appendonly yes"}, {"t": "Message Queues: RabbitMQ, SQS & Kafka - Web Servers, Databases & Messaging", "u": "13-webservers-databases/messaging-kafka.html", "x": "Services ko decouple karne ke liye async messaging. Queue = task distribution; Kafka = distributed log/event streaming. docker exec -it kafka /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-producer.sh --topic orders --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 docker exec -it kafka /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh --topic orders --from-beginning --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group billing --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 # LAG main queue --(fail x3)--> DLQ -> alert + manual/automatic replay"}, {"t": "SRE, Reliability & DevOps Culture", "u": "14-sre-culture/index.html", "x": "Tools se aage: reliability engineering, incident management, chaos testing, platform engineering aur DevOps mindset."}, {"t": "SRE, Reliability & DevOps Culture: Deep Guide", "u": "14-sre-culture/guide.html", "x": "Tools se aage: reliability engineering, incident management, chaos testing, platform engineering aur DevOps mindset. **Problem it solves:** Development wants to ship features fast; operations wants stability. When they are separate teams, they fight: releases are slow, incidents cause blame, and reliability is nobody's clear responsibility. **DevOps culture** and **Site Reliability Engineering (SRE)** unite building and running software: shared ownership, automation, measurable reliability targets (SLOs), blameless learning from incidents."}, {"t": "DevOps Culture, SDLC & Agile - SRE, Reliability & DevOps Culture", "u": "14-sre-culture/devops-culture-sdlc.html", "x": "DevOps = culture + practices + tools. Dev aur Ops ke beech ki deewar todna. CALMS: Culture, Automation, Lean, Measurement, Sharing. Scrum: sprints (2 wk), roles PO/SM/team, ceremonies (planning, standup, review, retro) Kanban: continuous flow, WIP limits, board columns"}, {"t": "Incident Management & Postmortems - SRE, Reliability & DevOps Culture", "u": "14-sre-culture/incident-management.html", "x": "Production down ho to panic nahi, process. Detect -> triage -> mitigate -> resolve -> blameless postmortem. MTTR kam karna goal hai. 1 Ack alert, open #inc-YYYYMMDD channel, assign Incident Commander 2 What changed? (deploys, config, traffic, dependency) -> check deploy annotations 3 Mitigate: rollback / scale up / failover / disable feature flag 4 Communicate status every 15-30 min; update status page 5 Verify recovery with SLI dashboards Summary | Impact (users, duration, $) | Timeline | Root cause (5 whys) | What went well/badly | Action items (owner, due) | Lessons"}, {"t": "Chaos Engineering & Load Testing - SRE, Reliability & DevOps Culture", "u": "14-sre-culture/chaos-load-testing.html", "x": "Failure ka wait mat karo, khud inject karo (controlled). Load test se capacity aur bottlenecks pata karo. import http from 'k6/http'; import { check, sleep } from 'k6'; export const options = { stages: [{ duration: '2m', target: 100 }, { duration: '5m', target: 100 }, { duration: '1m', target: 0 }], thresholds: { http_req_duration: ['p(95)<500'], http_req_failed: ['rate<0.01'] }, }; export default function () { const r = http.get('https://staging.example.com/api/products'); check(r, { 'status 200': (x) => x.status === 200 }); sleep(1); } apiVersion: chaos-mesh.org/v1alpha1 kind: PodChaos metad"}, {"t": "Platform Engineering, FinOps & Career - SRE, Reliability & DevOps Culture", "u": "14-sre-culture/platform-finops.html", "x": "Internal Developer Platform (golden paths), cost governance aur DevOps career path aur certifications. 0-6m Linux, Git, Docker, Bash/Python, AWS basics 6-12m CI/CD, Terraform, K8s, monitoring -> Junior DevOps / Cloud engineer 1-3y EKS in prod, GitOps, security, SRE practices -> DevOps Engineer 3y+ Architecture, platform eng, cost, leadership -> Senior/Staff/SRE/Architect Certs: AWS SAA -> CKA -> Terraform Associate -> CKS/AWS DevOps Pro 1 3-tier app on EKS w/ Terraform + ArgoCD 2 Full observability stack 3 Secure pipeline with scanning 4 Chaos + postmortem write-up Put README + architecture di"}, {"t": "HLD & LLD (Design + Diagrams + Real Examples)", "u": "hld-lld/index.html", "x": "HLD = bird's-eye view (components, flow, decisions). LLD = implementation detail (configs, code, commands). Interview mein 'design a CI/CD platform' jaise sawal aate hain, isliye dono practice karo."}, {"t": "HLD & LLD (Design + Diagrams + Real Examples): Deep Guide", "u": "hld-lld/guide.html", "x": "HLD = bird's-eye view (components, flow, decisions). LLD = implementation detail (configs, code, commands). Interview mein 'design a CI/CD platform' jaise sawal aate hain, isliye dono practice karo. **Problem it solves:** In senior interviews and real projects you are asked to *design* a system, not just use a tool: \"Design a highly available web app on AWS\", \"Design a CI/CD platform\". A good answer needs a repeatable method. **HLD (High-Level Design)** shows the big picture: components, flow and technology choices with reasons. **LLD (Low-Level Design)** shows the implementation detail: CIDRs"}, {"t": "How to Approach HLD & LLD - HLD & LLD (Design + Diagrams + Real Examples)", "u": "hld-lld/how-to-design.html", "x": "Design sawal ka framework: Requirements -> Constraints -> High-level diagram -> Components -> Data/Traffic flow -> Scale & HA -> Security -> Observability -> Cost -> Trade-offs. HLD mein 'kya aur kyun', LLD mein 'kaise'. Requirements -> Architecture diagram -> Component choices -> Scaling -> HA/DR -> Security -> Monitoring -> CI/CD -> Cost -> Trade-offs & risks flowchart LR U[User] --> CF[CloudFront] --> ALB --> APP[EKS pods] --> DB[(RDS)] APP --> R[(Redis)]"}, {"t": "HLD 1: Highly Available 3-Tier Web App on AWS - HLD & LLD (Design + Diagrams + Real Examples)", "u": "hld-lld/hld-3tier-aws.html", "x": "Sabse common design: users -> CDN -> ALB -> app tier (ASG/EKS) -> DB (Multi-AZ) with cache and object storage. CDN+WAF: latency, DDoS/OWASP protection ALB in public subnets, app in private: no direct internet access to compute Multi-AZ everywhere: survive AZ failure (RTO minutes) Redis: reduce DB load, session store RDS Multi-AZ + PITR backups: RPO ~5 min ASG target tracking 50% CPU: elastic scale VPC endpoints: private S3/ECR access, lower NAT cost Traffic 500 rps peak -> 4x t3.large (2/AZ), db.m6g.large Multi-AZ, cache.t3.medium Estimate via AWS Pricing Calculator; add Savings Plan after 1 m"}, {"t": "HLD 2: End-to-End CI/CD + GitOps Platform - HLD & LLD (Design + Diagrams + Real Examples)", "u": "hld-lld/hld-cicd-gitops-platform.html", "x": "Developer push se production tak. Build once, scan, sign, deploy via GitOps, observe and rollback. GitOps pull model: cluster creds never leave cluster Separate app repo and config repo: clean audit trail, different permissions Immutable tags (git sha) + image digest pinning Progressive delivery with automated metric analysis Promotion via PR between env folders Bad image -> caught by scan gate | Bad config -> canary aborts, ArgoCD rollback ArgoCD down -> running workloads unaffected, deployments pause | GitHub down -> hotfix via break-glass procedure"}, {"t": "HLD 3: Observability & Alerting Platform - HLD & LLD (Design + Diagrams + Real Examples)", "u": "hld-lld/hld-observability-platform.html", "x": "Central monitoring for many services/clusters: metrics, logs, traces, alerts with SLOs. Prometheus 15d local + remote_write to Mimir 13 months Loki 14d hot, S3 90d ; Traces 7d with tail-sampling (errors+slow 100%, rest 5%) SLI availability + latency p95, error budget remaining, burn rate, deploy markers, top failing endpoints"}, {"t": "LLD 1: VPC Subnets, CIDR & Security Group Rules - HLD & LLD (Design + Diagrams + Real Examples)", "u": "hld-lld/lld-vpc-cidr-sg.html", "x": "HLD ka network ab exact numbers ke saath. sg-alb : in 443 from 0.0.0.0/0 ; out all sg-app : in 3000 from sg-alb ; out 5432 to sg-db, 6379 to sg-redis, 443 to 0.0.0.0/0 (via NAT) sg-db : in 5432 from sg-app ; no outbound needed sg-bastion/SSM: prefer SSM Session Manager, no inbound 22 resource \"aws_security_group_rule\" \"db_from_app\" { type = \"ingress\" from_port = 5432 to_port = 5432 protocol = \"tcp\" security_group_id = aws_security_group.db.id source_security_group_id = aws_security_group.app.id }"}, {"t": "LLD 2: CI Pipeline, Dockerfile & Deploy Config - HLD & LLD (Design + Diagrams + Real Examples)", "u": "hld-lld/lld-cicd-pipeline.html", "x": "Exact GitHub Actions workflow, Dockerfile aur GitOps tag update ka implementation. name: ci on: { push: { branches: [main] }, pull_request: } permissions: { id-token: write, contents: read } env: { REGION: ap-south-1, REPO: myapp } jobs: build: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - run: npm ci && npm test - uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@master with: { scan-type: fs, severity: 'HIGH,CRITICAL', exit-code: '1' } - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4 with: { role-to-assume: \"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/gha-ecr\", aws-region: \"${{ env.REGION }}\" } - id: ecr uses"}, {"t": "LLD 3: Kubernetes App Manifests (Production Grade) - HLD & LLD (Design + Diagrams + Real Examples)", "u": "hld-lld/lld-k8s-app-manifests.html", "x": "Deployment + Service + Ingress + HPA + PDB + NetworkPolicy + ServiceAccount: production checklist wala YAML. apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: { name: myapp, namespace: prod } spec: replicas: 3 strategy: { type: RollingUpdate, rollingUpdate: { maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0 } } selector: { matchLabels: { app: myapp } } template: metadata: { labels: { app: myapp } } spec: serviceAccountName: myapp securityContext: { runAsNonRoot: true, seccompProfile: { type: RuntimeDefault } } topologySpreadConstraints: - { maxSkew: 1, topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone, whenUnsatisfiable: S"}, {"t": "LLD 4: Alert Rules, Dashboards & Runbook - HLD & LLD (Design + Diagrams + Real Examples)", "u": "hld-lld/lld-monitoring-alerts-runbook.html", "x": "Exact alert rules, dashboard panels aur runbook jo on-call engineer follow karega. apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1 kind: PrometheusRule metadata: { name: myapp, namespace: monitoring, labels: { release: mon } } spec: groups: - name: myapp.slo rules: - alert: MyAppHighErrorRate expr: sum(rate(http_requests_total{app=\"myapp\",code=~\"5..\"}[5m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total{app=\"myapp\"}[5m])) > 0.02 for: 5m labels: { severity: page, team: backend } annotations: { summary: \"myapp 5xx > 2%\", runbook_url: \"https://wiki/runbooks/myapp-5xx\" } - alert: MyAppLatencyHigh expr: histogram_quantile(0."}, {"t": "Real Problem Walkthroughs (Scenario Practice) - HLD & LLD (Design + Diagrams + Real Examples)", "u": "hld-lld/real-problem-walkthroughs.html", "x": "Interview aur job mein aane wale real scenarios: symptom -> investigation steps -> fix -> prevention. Scope: p95 latency 3s, errors normal, started 10:05 = deploy time Check: Grafana latency by endpoint -> /search slow; traces show DB query 2.5s Cause: new query missing index (EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows seq scan) Mitigate: rollback | Fix: CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY | Prevent: query perf test in CI, slow query alert logs --previous: 'DB_HOST undefined' Cause: ConfigMap key renamed but Deployment still references old key Fix: correct env mapping, roll out | Prevent: helm template + kubeconform in CI, "}];